  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag98" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;!--?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?--&gt;
&lt;div style="line-height: 108%; font-family: Calibri; font-size: 11pt;"&gt;
&lt;div class="Section_1"&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. ORGANIZATION, BUSINESS OPERATIONS AND BASIS OF PRESENTATION&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Organization. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Double E Pipeline, LLC ("Double E" or the "Company"), a Delaware limited liability company, was formed on June 4, 2019. Subsequently, on June 26, 2019, Summit Permian Transmission, LLC ("Permian Transmission"), a wholly owned subsidiary of Summit Midstream Corporation ("SMC"), and ExxonMobil Permian Double E Pipeline LLC (the "JV Partner") entered into an Amended and Restated Limited Liability Company Agreement of Double E Pipeline, LLC (the "Agreement") on June 26, 2019. In connection with the Agreement, Summit Midstream Partners, LP (&amp;#8220;SMLP&amp;#8221;) and the JV Partner obtained a 70% and 30% ownership interest, respectively, in Double E. References to the "Company," "Double E," "we," or "our" refer collectively to Double E.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;Double E owns a long-haul natural gas pipeline that provides transportation service from multiple receipt points in the Delaware Basin to various delivery points in and around the Waha Hub in Texas (the "Project"). The Pipeline was placed into service and began flowing natural gas in November 2021.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;In December 2019, Summit Permian Transmission Holdco, LLC ("Permian Holdco"), a wholly owned subsidiary of SMLP, became the 100% owner of Permian Transmission. Through its ownership in Permian Transmission, Permian Holdco indirectly owns Permian Transmission's 70% interest in Double E.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;Summit Midstream Permian II, LLC ("Summit Permian II"), a wholly owned subsidiary of SMLP and 100% owner of Permian Holdco, led the development, permitting and construction of the Project and operates the pipeline on behalf of Double E.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Basis of Presentation and Principles of Consolidation.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company prepares its financial statements in accordance with accounting principles of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in the Uniform System of accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America ("GAAP").&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;The most significant differences between GAAP and the financial statements presented herein are: (i) the classification of certain accounts on the balance sheet, statement of income and statement of cash flows, (ii) the inclusion and classification of income taxes in a limited liability company's financial statements, and (iii) the carrying cost AFUDC on debt and equity funds related to the construction of long-lived assets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. SUMMARY OF SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company considers all highly liquid investments with an original maturity of three months or less to be cash equivalents.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Accounts Receivable&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Accounts receivable relate primarily to transportation services provided to the Company's customers. Historically, we have not experienced material losses related to our accounts receivable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Property and Equipment.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company records property and equipment at historical cost of construction or fair value of the assets at acquisition.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;The Company records depreciation on a straight-line basis over an asset's FERC approved estimated useful life and bases its estimates for useful life on various factors including age (in the case of acquired assets), manufacturing specifications, technological advances and historical data concerning useful lives of similar assets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;The Company capitalizes a carrying cost (an allowance for funds used during construction or AFUDC) on debt and equity funds related to the construction of long-lived assets. This carrying cost consists of a return on the investment financed by debt and a return on the investment financed by equity. The debt portion is calculated based on our most recent FERC approved debt rate. Interest costs capitalized are included as a reduction to interest charges in Account 432 "Allowance for Borrowed Funds Used During Construction - Credit" on our income statements. The equity portion is calculated based on our most recent FERC approved rate of return. Equity amounts capitalized are included in Account 419.1 "Allowance for Other Funds Used During Construction" on our income statements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Natural Gas Imbalances.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Natural gas imbalances occur when the amount of natural gas delivered from or received by a pipeline system or storage facility differs from the scheduled amount of gas to be delivered or received. The Company values these imbalances due to or from shippers and operators at current index prices. Imbalances are settled in cash or made up in-kind, subject to the terms of the Company's Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") tariff. Imbalances due from others are reported on our balance sheets as part of "Other Current Assets." Imbalances owed to others are reported on our balance sheets as part of "Trade Accounts Payable." The Company classifies all imbalances due from or owed to others as current as they are expected to settle within one year.&amp;#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Revenue Recognition.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Revenue from Contracts with Customers.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;The Company reviews its contracts with customers using the following steps to recognize revenue based on the transfer of goods or services to customers and in amounts that reflect the consideration the Company expects to receive for those goods or services. The steps include: (i) identify the contract; (ii) identify the performance obligations of the contract; (iii) determine the transaction price; (iv) allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations in the contract; and then (v) recognize revenue when (or as) the performance obligation is satisfied. Each of these steps involves management judgment and an analysis of the contract's material terms and conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Natural Gas Transportation Contracts.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;The Company's revenues are primarily generated from the transportation of natural gas under firm service customer contracts with take-or-pay elements (principally for capacity reservation) where both the price and quantity are fixed. Generally, for these contracts: (i) our obligation is to transfer (or stand ready to transfer) a series of distinct integrated services over a period of time, which is a single performance obligation; (ii) the transaction price includes fixed and/or variable consideration, which is determinable at contract inception and/or at each month end based on our right to invoice at month end for the value of services provided to the customer that month; and (iii) the transaction price is recognized as revenue over the service period specified in the contract as the services are rendered. In these arrangements, the customer is obligated to pay for services associated with its take-or-pay obligation regardless of whether or not the customer chooses to utilize the service in that period. Because we make the service continuously available over the service period, we recognize the take-or-pay amount as revenue ratably over the period based on the passage of time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;The natural gas the Company receives under its transportation contracts remains under the control of its customers. In many cases, generally described as firm service, the customer generally pays a two-part transaction price that includes (i) a fixed fee reserving the right to transport natural gas in our facilities up to contractually specified capacity levels (referred to as "reservation") and (ii) a fee-based per-unit rate for quantities of natural gas actually transported. In the Company's firm service contracts they generally promise to provide a single integrated service each day over the life of the contract, which is fundamentally a stand-ready obligation to provide services up to the customer's reservation capacity prescribed in the contract. The Company's customers have a take-or-pay payment obligation with respect to the fixed reservation fee component, regardless of the quantities actually transported. In other cases, generally described as interruptible service, there is no fixed fee associated with these transportation services because the customer accepts the possibility that service may be interrupted at the Company's discretion in order to serve customers who have firm service contracts. The Company does not have an obligation to perform under interruptible customer arrangements until it accepts and schedules the customer's request for periodic service. The customer pays a transaction price on a fee-based per-unit rate for the quantities actually transported.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Deferred Revenue. &lt;/em&gt;The balances in deferred revenue are primarily related to contributions in aid of construction which will be recognized as revenue over the life of the contract.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Related Party Revenues.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;For the three and six months ended June 30, 2026, revenues from related parties totaled $9.5 million and $18.9 million, respectively. For the three and six months ended June 30, 2025, revenues from related parties totaled $9.2 million and $18.3 million, respectively. These amounts are related to firm transportation agreements with XTO Energy, Inc. (&amp;#8220;XTO&amp;#8221;) a subsidiary of ExxonMobil. As of June 30, 2026 and June 30, 2025, trade receivable balances from XTO totaled $3.1 million and $2.9 million, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company records a liability for asset retirement obligations only if and when a future asset retirement obligation with a determinable life is identified. For identified asset retirement obligations, the Company then evaluates whether the expected date and related costs of retirement can be estimated. The Company concluded that these assets have an indeterminate life because they are owned and will operate for an indeterminate period when properly maintained. Because there are no current plans to discontinue use of any significant assets, the Company did not provide for any asset retirement obligations as of June 30, 2026 and 2025.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Amortizing Intangibles.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company has easements granted within existing rights-of-way. The intangible assets will be amortized over the shorter of the contractual term of the rights-of-way or the estimated useful life of the pipeline. The contractual terms of the rights-of-way range from 10 years to terms in perpetuity. Amortization expense associated with right-of-way assets is recognized in depreciation and amortization expense within the Statements of Operations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company tests assets for impairment when events or circumstances indicate that the carrying value of a long-lived asset may not be recoverable. The carrying value of a long-lived asset is not recoverable if it exceeds the sum of the undiscounted cash flows expected to result from its use and eventual disposition. If the Company concludes that an asset's carrying value will not be recovered through future cash flows, an impairment loss is recognized on the long-lived asset equal to the amount by which the carrying value exceeds its fair value. Fair value is determined utilizing a market-based approach, an income-based approach or a combination of the two approaches. Any such impairment losses at Double E would be recorded as a regulatory asset until regulatory review regarding recoverability through the rate-making process is complete, at which time the Company will recognize the loss if it is determined to be unrecoverable or retain it as a regulatory asset and recover through its rates.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Commitments and Contingencies.&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The Company records accruals for loss contingencies when it determines that it is probable that a liability has been incurred and that such economic loss can be reasonably estimated. Such determinations are subject to interpretations of current facts and circumstances, forecasts of future events and estimates of the financial impacts of such events. Gain contingencies are recognized when their realization is assured beyond a reasonable doubt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. INCOME TAXES&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;Double E is treated as a partnership for income tax purposes and does not pay federal or state income taxes. However, since federal and state income taxes have historically been a cost component in rates allowed by the FERC, these taxes are included in the financial statements. The computation of the deferred income tax expense assumes the use of an accelerated method for calculating tax depreciation. Double E records deferred income taxes for significant differences between book and taxable income. The Members report on their separate federal income tax returns the appropriate share of revenues and expenses. On March 15, 2018, in Docket No. PL17-1-000, the FERC issued a revised Policy Statement finding that interstate natural gas pipelines held by Master Limited Partnerships are no longer permitted to recover an income tax allowance in their cost of service.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;Deferred income taxes charged to Double E and assignable to the Members are reflected in the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheet as accumulated deferred income taxes (Accounts 190, 282 and 283).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal"&gt;In 2026 and 2025, substantially all of the deferred income tax expense applicable to Double E was related to federal income taxes. Deferred income taxes for the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025 resulted in the recognition of $3.1 million and $2.6 million, respectively of deferred income tax expense at a federal income tax rate of 21%.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
